# Banking Partner Web Architecture Rules

## 1. Purpose

This repository contains the Partner Web microfrontend.

Its responsibility is to provide the web interface used by Santander partners to search customers and start a banking request.

This microfrontend owns only the Partner Portal experience.

It must not implement onboarding logic, contracts logic, loan orchestration or business rules belonging to downstream microfrontends.

---

# 2. Design Philosophy

The UI must prioritize:

- clean banking interface
- pixel-perfect implementation
- responsive desktop-first layout
- reusable UI components
- consistent spacing
- accessibility
- production-ready code

The provided UI mockups are the design source of truth.

Cursor should reproduce the design as accurately as possible.

---

# 3. Desktop First

This project targets desktop browsers.

Desktop layout is the primary implementation.

Rules

- design for desktop first
- minimum width 1280px
- optimize for 1366px and 1920px screens
- responsive behavior is secondary
- avoid mobile-first assumptions

---

# 4. Page Ownership

This repository owns pages such as:

- customer search
- customer result
- request initialization
- request tracking
- dashboard
- shared layouts

Future pages must remain inside the Partner Portal domain.

---

# 5. Layout Structure

Every page should follow the same layout hierarchy.

PartnerLayout

├── Header

├── Main Content

│      ├── Page Title

│      ├── Description

│      ├── Feature Container

│      └── Action Area

└── Footer (if required)

Layout must remain consistent across the application.

---

# 6. Header

The header must be implemented as an independent reusable component.

Responsibilities

- Santander logo
- navigation menu
- authenticated user information
- notifications
- logout button

Header must not contain page-specific logic.

---

# 7. Page Composition

Pages should be composed of reusable containers instead of large components.

Example

CustomerSearchPage

├── CustomerSearchCard

├── CustomerResultCard

├── EmptyState

├── LoadingState

└── ErrorState

Avoid large monolithic page components.

---

# 8. Component Organization

Follow feature-first architecture.

Example

features/

    customer-search/

        pages/

        components/

        services/

        models/

        state/

        guards/

        interfaces/

        utils/

Shared components belong inside

shared/

Examples

Button

Input

Select

Card

Badge

Dialog

Loader

Empty State

---

# 9. Design System

All UI must reuse the Santander design language.

Spacing

- 4
- 8
- 12
- 16
- 24
- 32
- 40
- 48

Border radius

consistent

Shadows

soft

Cards

rounded

Avoid hardcoded styles duplicated across components.

---

# 10. Forms

Forms must use

Reactive Forms

Every field must have

- validation
- disabled state
- loading state
- error state

Form logic must remain outside presentation components.

---

# 11. State Management

Each feature owns its own reactive state.

State should include

- loading
- success
- error
- current customer
- search result
- request status

Avoid global mutable variables.

---

# 12. Services

Separate responsibilities.

Examples

CustomerApiService

BranchApiService

RequestApiService

PartnerApiService

Do not mix unrelated endpoints in a single service.

---

# 13. Routing

Routes must be feature-oriented.

Example

/customer-search

/request

/tracking

/dashboard

Avoid generic routes.

---

# 14. Naming

Everything must be written in English.

Preferred examples

customer-search-page.component.ts

customer-search-card.component.ts

customer-api.service.ts

request-state.service.ts

partner-layout.component.ts

Avoid Spanish names.

---

# 15. Styling

Prefer SCSS.

Follow BEM methodology.

Avoid inline styles.

Avoid duplicated CSS.

Every component owns its own SCSS file.

---

# 16. Reusability

If a UI block appears more than once, create a reusable component.

Examples

Search Card

Information Card

Status Card

Result Card

Confirmation Card

Buttons

Inputs

Selectors

---

# 17. Performance

Prefer

OnPush change detection

Signals

Standalone Components

Lazy-loaded features

Avoid unnecessary subscriptions.

---

# 18. Error Handling

Every feature should support

Loading

Empty

Error

Success

Do not leave blank pages.

---

# 19. Accessibility

Components should include

keyboard navigation

focus management

ARIA labels

semantic HTML

---

# 20. Visual Consistency

Cursor must reproduce the provided mockups as closely as possible.

Pay special attention to

- spacing
- alignment
- typography
- colors
- shadows
- border radius
- card sizes
- button proportions
- icon placement
- header spacing

Pixel-perfect implementation is preferred over approximate layouts.

---

# 21. Cursor Responsibilities

When generating code, Cursor should

- preserve project architecture
- keep features isolated
- create reusable components
- avoid duplicated logic
- generate scalable folder structures
- produce production-ready Angular code
- follow Angular 20 best practices
- use standalone components
- use Signals where appropriate
- keep UI modular and maintainable

---

# 22. Cursor Should Avoid

Do not

- generate giant page components
- duplicate UI
- mix business logic with presentation
- hardcode layout values repeatedly
- couple independent features
- create generic service names
- use inline CSS
- create unnecessary wrappers
- generate temporary demo code